Main things for the planner
1. #1202, dynamic shell words (the retry of #1205). Codex shipped the exact fix in PR #39159. tree-sitter shows brace expansion, globs and backslash escapes as ordinary words, so Codex stops treating unquoted words containing them as literal and asks for approval even when an allow rule matches. Its tests use only harmless commands (find . -{delete,print}, echo HEAD~1, echo foo#bar, plus quoted forms that should still count as literal). That gives us a ready test table, and it avoids the long list of disk-wiping commands that got the Day 261 task refused.
2. #1202, the owner's bolded "fails closed" rule. When a command can't be parsed, or uses eval or a command name built from a variable, yoyo still falls back to text-only checks instead of asking first. Claude Code 2.1.326 (released Nov 16) keeps moving the same way: when its checker can't prove a command is safe, it asks.
3. #1184 next slice. The [permissions] and [directories] arrays are still read by the hand-written line reader, and those are the security fence.
4. File-size limit. src/context.rs is 15 lines away from failing the module-size test, and src/format/mod.rs is 14 lines away. A task that touches either one has to update its entry in tests/module_size.rs in the same change, or the whole task gets reverted.

